const e={type:"config",goal:"13",title:"Exposure, Vulnerability, and Adaptation to Climate Hazards",shortTitle:"Climate",abstract:"Extreme weather events, such as floods, droughts, storms, and heatwaves are more frequent and stronger due to climate change. They impact more than half the world’s population, almost one-third of whom  are in highly vulnerable conditions and must adapt to a changing climate.",citation:'Benschop, T, Groves, D, Lønborg, J H and Rininsland, Æ. 2026. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.60616/d3qh-6653" target="_blank" rel="external">Exposure, Vulnerability and Adaptation to Climate Hazards</a>.” In Atlas of Global Development 2026, edited by A F Pirlea, D Wadhwa, D Mahler, U Serajuddin, M Welch, A Thudt, and M Lambrechts. Washington DC: World Bank. License: Creative Commons Attribution CC BY 3.0 IGO. ',credits:"Thijs Benschop, Jonas Helth Lønborg & David Groves",inputs:"Jia Li, Esther Naikal, Minh Cong Nguyen",visuals:"Ændra Rininsland",homeFactoidNumber:"1 in 5",homeFactoidPercent:"18",homeFactoidText:"people are exposed and vulnerable to climate hazards",homeTagline:"Climate change intensifies extreme weather events, exposing more than half the global population, a large share of which is also vulnerable and in need of adaptation.",theme:"planet",publicationDate:"2026-05-05",slug:"climate",keyFacts:[{_key:"k8261",contentBlockKey:"c19",keyFact:"4.5 billion",keyFactLabel:"people are exposed to climate-related hazards"},{_key:"k8384",contentBlockKey:"c25",keyFact:"2.8 billion",keyFactLabel:"people live in highly vulnerable conditions globally"},{_key:"k8503",contentBlockKey:"c31",keyFact:"1.5 billion",keyFactLabel:"people are exposed to climate-related hazards and living in highly vulnerable conditions"}]},a=[{_key:"k7894",type:"subhed",text:"Climate change affects people, livelihoods, and the economy"},{_key:"k7905",type:"text",text:`Climate change affects natural and human systems as temperatures rise, rainfall patterns shift, and extreme weather events—such as heatwaves, floods, droughts, and storms—become more frequent and intense.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_1_1683949207' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_1_1683949207'/><div class="details">IPCC. 2022. “<a href="https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg2/" target="_blank" rel="external">Climate Change 2022: Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability. Contribution of Working Group II to the Sixth Assessment Report</a>.” Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.</div></div> These changes disrupt ecosystems and economies alike. Prolonged droughts reduce agricultural yields,<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 2"><label for='reference_2_1_1499520097' aria-hidden="true">2</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_2_1_1499520097'/><div class="details">FAO. “<a href="https://www.fao.org/land-water/water/drought/droughtandag/en/" target="_blank" rel="external">Drought and agriculture.</a>” Land & Water.  Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. </div></div> floods destroy homes and infrastructure, and heat stress threatens health and productivity.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_1_900006750' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_1_900006750'/><div class="details">ILO. 2019. “<a href="https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@dgreports/@dcomm/@publ/documents/publication/wcms_711919.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Working on a Warmer Planet: The Impact of Heat Stress on Labour Productivity and Decent Work</a>.” Geneva: International Labour Organization.</div></div>`},{_key:"k7910",type:"text",text:`These impacts are not felt equally. People in different locations face different levels of exposure to climate hazards. But even when faced with the same hazard, some people are more likely to be adversely affected or unable to cope with its impacts— for example, if they live in poverty or lack access to social protection.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 4"><label for='reference_4_1_659911920' aria-hidden="true">4</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_4_1_659911920'/><div class="details">Abeygunawardena, P, Vyas, Y, Knill, P, Foy, T, Harrold, M, Steele, P, Tanner, T, Hirsch, D, Oosterman, M, Rooimans, J, Debois, M, Lamin, M, Liptow, H, Mausolf, E, Verheyen, R, Agrawala, S, Caspary, G, Paris, R, Kashyap, A, Sharma, A, Mathur, A, Sharma, M and Sperling, F. 2010. “<a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/534871468155709473" target="_blank" rel="external">Poverty and Climate Change: Reducing the Vulnerability of the Poor through Adaptation.</a>“ Washington DC: World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"k7913",type:"text",text:"Let’s see which climate hazards the world’s population is exposed to and what makes people vulnerable."},{_key:"k7914",type:"subhed",text:"Exposure to climate hazards"},{_key:"k7917",type:"text",text:"Not all places are equally exposed to climate-related hazards, and the number of people living in affected areas differs substantially. So, to estimate the number of exposed people per country, we need to overlay the geographical location of the hazards with the number of people living there."},{_key:"k7935",type:"text",text:`Around 4.5 billion people were exposed to certain climate-related hazards in 2024.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 5"><label for='reference_5_1_1449623743' aria-hidden="true">5</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_5_1_1449623743'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2024. “<a href="https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/rising-to-the-challenge-climate-adaptation-resilience" target="_blank" rel="external">Rising to the Challenge: Success Stories and Strategies for Achieving Climate Adaptation and Resilience</a>.” Washington DC: World Bank. </div></div> Exposed population means the people living in an area affected by a given climate-related hazard. An area is considered exposed if the probability that a hazard strikes in a given year is more than 1 percent. This translates into a  probability of slightly over 50 percent<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1305076884_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1305076884_1'/><div class="details">To estimate exposure, we consider a 100-year return period, which is the same as a 1 percent probability that the hazard occurs in a given year. Average global life expectancy is 72 years. An event with a probability of annual occurrence of 0.01 has a probability of 1-(1-0.01)^72=0.515 or 52 percent of occurring in a 72-year period.</div></div> that a person faces the hazard over their lifespan. Weather events are considered hazards when their intensity is high enough to significantly impact lives, assets, and livelihoods.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1600669233_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1600669233_1'/><div class="details">We use hazard-specific thresholds to determine whether a climate-related hazard has a significant impact. For instance, for floods, we use a threshold for inundation depth, and for cyclones, a threshold for wind speed.</div></div>`},{_key:"k7936",type:"factoid",title:"Population exposed to selected climate-related hazards",number:"4.5",units:"billion people",label:"globally in 2024"},{_key:"k7961",type:"text",text:'While there are many climate-related hazards, we focus on <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods</strong></span>,  <span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>agricultural droughts</strong></span>, <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)"><strong>cyclones </strong></span> and <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>heatwaves</strong></span>. Let’s explore the number of people exposed to each of these climate-related hazards.'},{_key:"k7971",type:"text",text:`<span data-color-id="cat1" class="cat1"><strong>Floods</strong></span> occur because of heavy rain, overflowing rivers, and rising sea levels. Climate change affects precipitation patterns, snow and ice melt, and  sea level rise, leading to more frequent and heavier floods.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 6"><label for='reference_6_1_86969815' aria-hidden="true">6</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_6_1_86969815'/><div class="details">UNEP. 2020. “<a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/story/how-climate-change-making-record-breaking-floods-new-normal" target="_blank" rel="external">How Climate Change is Making Record-breaking Floods the New Normal</a><a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/story/how-climate-change-making-record-breaking-floods-new-normal" target="_blank" rel="external">.</a>” United Nations Environment Program climate action story, March 3.</div></div> `},{_key:"c10",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller",title:"775 million people are exposed to floods globally",subtitle:`Area and share of population exposed to floods with inundation depth over 50cm<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_343611355_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_343611355_1'/><div class="details">An area is exposed to floods if there is more than a 1 percent annual probability of  experiencing flooding with inundation depth of more than  50 centimeters.</div></div>`,source:'<a href="https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/deltares-floods" target="_blank" rel="external">Deltares</a>, <a href="https://www.fathom.global/product/global-flood-map/" target="_blank" rel="external">Fathom</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Share of population exposed to floods and location of areas exposed to flood risk",data:"1b0tjeAlBqbSgpOKmVE4xMafDRNvsUWG7_C_jDMsorFI",anchorKey:"c10",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,weatherEventToggleText:"Exposed areas",legend_title:"Population exposed",legend_units:"%",gallery_thumbnail:"climate_sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller",scenes:[{_key:"k7972",type:"scene",id:"floods__sat__wld",text:`<span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>Floods</strong></span> can severely damage infrastructure and homes, disrupt the economy, and destroy crops and livestock. Impacts become particularly severe when inundation depth exceeds 50 centimeters.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 7"><label for='reference_7_1_895485272' aria-hidden="true">7</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_7_1_895485272'/><div class="details">Rentschler, J, Salhab, M and Jafino, B A. 2022. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-30727-4" target="_blank" rel="external">Flood Exposure and Poverty in 188 Countries.</a>” Nature Communications 13: 3527.</div></div> The areas in <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>blue</strong></span> indicate exposure above this level.`},{_key:"k7987",type:"scene",id:"floods__sat__bbox1",text:'The <strong>population exposed to</strong> <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods</strong></span> live near rivers, in areas with low elevation, and near the coast. In The Netherlands, more than one-third of the population is exposed to <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods</strong></span> due to the country’s  many rivers and low elevation.'},{_key:"k8004",type:"scene",id:"floods__vec__wld",text:'Globally, <strong>775 million people are exposed to</strong> <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods.</strong></span> Half of them live in three populous countries: China, India, and Bangladesh. In Bangladesh, one-third of the population is exposed to <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods</strong></span>.'}]},{_key:"k8046",type:"text",text:'<span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>Agricultural droughts</strong></span> occur when there is not enough soil moisture and water availability to meet the needs of crops and livestock, reducing agricultural production and undermining both food security and rural livelihoods. Climate change is intensifying such droughts by increasing their frequency, severity, and duration, placing growing pressure on already vulnerable farming systems.'},{_key:"c12",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller",title:"Globally, almost 1.4 billion people in rural areas are exposed to agricultural drought ",subtitle:`Areas and share of population exposed to agricultural drought events.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_702006333_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_702006333_1'/><div class="details">An area is defined as exposed to agricultural drought if at least once in the last 39 years more than 30 percent of cropland or pasture is lost due to drought.</div></div>`,source:'<a href="https://www.fao.org/giews/earthobservation/asis/index_1.jsp?lang=en" target="_blank" rel="external">Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Share of population exposed to agricultural drought and location of areas exposed to agricultural drought.",data:"1b0tjeAlBqbSgpOKmVE4xMafDRNvsUWG7_C_jDMsorFI",anchorKey:"c12",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,weatherEventToggleText:"Exposed areas",legend_title:"Population exposed",legend_units:"%",scenes:[{_key:"k8047",type:"scene",id:"drought__sat__wld",text:`An <strong>area</strong> is defined as exposed to <span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>agricultural drought</strong></span> if more than 30 percent of cropland or pasture experienced severe drought in at least one year in the past 39 years.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_846238628_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_846238628_1'/><div class="details">Measured using the FAO’s Agricultural Stress Index .<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 8"><label for='reference_8_1_739440336' aria-hidden="true">8</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_8_1_739440336'/><div class="details">FAO. <a href="https://www.fao.org/giews/earthobservation/asis/index_1.jsp?lang=en" target="_blank" rel="external">“Agricultural Stress Index System (ASIS).</a>” Earth Observation. Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations.</div></div> <div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 9"><label for='reference_9_1_97824982' aria-hidden="true">9</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_9_1_97824982'/><div class="details">Van Hoolst, R, Eerens, H, Haesen, D, Royer, A, Bydekerke, L, Rojas, O, Li, Y and Racionzer, P. 2016. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/01431161.2015.1126378" target="_blank" rel="external">FAO’s AVHRR-based Agricultural Stress Index System (ASIS) for Global Drought Monitoring</a>.” International Journal of Remote Sensing 37(2): 418–439.</div></div></div></div> When this threshold is exceeded, agricultural production losses are expected. <strong>Land affected by agricultural droughts has more than doubled in the last 40 years.</strong>`},{_key:"k8073",type:"scene",id:"drought__sat__bbox1",text:'<span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>Agricultural drought</strong></span> is most prevalent in arid and semi-arid regions. In Eswatini, Lesotho, Namibia, and Zimbabwe, more than half the population is exposed to drought.'},{_key:"k8080",type:"scene",id:"drought__vec__wld",text:`Globally, almost 1.4 billion people are exposed to <span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>agricultural drought</strong></span><div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 10"><label for='reference_10_1_1728418124' aria-hidden="true">10</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_10_1_1728418124'/><div class="details">Hill, R, Gorgulu, N, Brunckhorst, B, Nguyen, M, Hallegatte, S and Naikal, E. 2025. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">One-fifth of the World’s Population at High Risk from C</a>l<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">imate-related Hazards.” </a>(Preprint). Research Square.</div></div>, which can halt or reverse progress in poverty reduction and food security. Beyond agriculture, prolonged droughts also contribute to environmental degradation, forced migration, and heightened social tensions.`}]},{_key:"k8117",type:"text",text:`<span data-color-id="cat3" class="cat3"><strong>Cyclones</strong></span>, also known as hurricanes or typhoons, are large revolving storms that form over tropical oceans.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_2138438597_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_2138438597_1'/><div class="details">These are known by different names, depending on their location. Over the Atlantic and East Pacific Oceans, they are commonly called hurricanes; in the West Pacific Ocean, they are referred to as typhoons; and in the Indian Ocean and around Australia, they are called cyclones.</div></div> Climate change causes ocean water temperatures to rise, increasing the strength of cyclones and resulting in heavier rainfall. `},{_key:"c15",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller",title:"Globally, almost 8 percent of the population is exposed to cyclones",subtitle:`Area and share of population exposed to cyclones of category 2 or higher<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1285017777_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1285017777_1'/><div class="details">The strength of a cyclone is assessed on a scale of 1–5 using the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 11"><label for='reference_11_1_1964398285' aria-hidden="true">11</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_11_1_1964398285'/><div class="details">National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mfl/saffirsimpson" target="_blank" rel="external">“Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Scale</a><a href="https://www.weather.gov/mfl/saffirsimpson" target="_blank" rel="external">”</a>.  </div></div>. Cyclones with wind speeds over 154 km/h are Category 2+.</div></div>`,source:'<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-020-0381-2" target="_blank" rel="external">Bloemendaal et al.</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Share of population exposed to cyclones and location of areas exposed to cyclones ",data:"1b0tjeAlBqbSgpOKmVE4xMafDRNvsUWG7_C_jDMsorFI",anchorKey:"c15",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,weatherEventToggleText:"Exposed areas",legend_title:"Population exposed",legend_units:"%",scenes:[{_key:"k8118",type:"scene",id:"cyclones__sat__wld",text:`<span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)"><strong>Cyclones</strong></span> of category 2 or above<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_718695272_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_718695272_1'/><div class="details">Cyclones are measured on the Saffir Simpson scale, which classifies them into five categories (1–5) based on their maximum sustained wind speed. Cyclones with wind speeds over 154 km/h are classified as Category 2 and above and can cause extensive damage.</div></div> are associated with extensive damage to buildings and infrastructure. `},{_key:"k8130",type:"scene",id:"cyclones__vec__bbox1",text:`Tropical <span data-color-id="cat3" class="cat3"><strong>cyclone</strong></span> Melissa made landfall in Jamaica on October 28,  2025. The third most intense <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)"><strong>cyclone</strong></span> ever recorded in the Atlantic Ocean, it was classified as a category 5 and had wind speeds of up to 300 km/h.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 12"><label for='reference_12_1_1318278813' aria-hidden="true">12</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_12_1_1318278813'/><div class="details">WMO. 2025. “<a href="https://wmo.int/media/news/category-5-hurricane-melissa-hits-jamaica-and-caribbean-islands" target="_blank" rel="external">Category 5 Hurricane Melissa hits Jamaica and Caribbean islands.</a>” World Meteorological Organization News, October 28.</div></div> Although it wreaked enormous  damage on infrastructure and houses, due to early warning systems, the number of casualties was limited at 50.`},{_key:"k8146",type:"scene",id:"cyclones__vec__wld",text:`<span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)"><strong>Cyclones</strong></span> occur over warm tropical oceans, exposing people in their proximity. In many Small Island Developing States, the entire population is exposed: in 36  Caribbean and Pacific economies,<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1209471730_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1209471730_1'/><div class="details">Aruba, Anguilla, American Samoa, Antigua and Barbuda, Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba, Bahamas, St. Barthélemy, Bermuda, Barbados, Cuba, Curaçao, Cayman Islands, Dominica, Dominican Republic, Grenada, Guam, Haiti, Jamaica, St, Kitts and Nevis, St. Lucia, St. Martin (French part), Northern Mariana Islands, Montserrat, Mauritius, Niue, Puerto Rico, Sint Maarten (Dutch part), Turks and Caicos Islands, Tonga, Taiwan, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, British Virgin Islands, United States Virgin Islands, Vanuatu, Wallis and Futuna, and Samoa. In Fiji, more than 99 percent of the population is exposed.</div></div> 100 percent of the population is exposed. `}]},{_key:"k8188",type:"text",text:`<span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>Heatwaves</strong></span> are prolonged periods of unusually high temperatures. Heatwaves can impact human health through an increase in the risk of dehydration, heat exhaustion and heatstroke, and worsen cardiovascular and respiratory diseases. This significantly raises mortality, especially among older adults and people with pre-existing conditions.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 13"><label for='reference_13_1_499808447' aria-hidden="true">13</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_13_1_499808447'/><div class="details">WHO. “<a href="https://www.who.int/health-topics/heatwaves" target="_blank" rel="external">Heatwaves.</a>” World Health Organization.  </div></div> Heatwaves can also reduce economic output, as labor productivity becomes more strenuous, particularly in outdoor activities such as agriculture, construction, and manufacturing. They also increase demand for electricity, and put additional stress on infrastructure and health care systems.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_2_900006750' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_2_900006750'/><div class="details">ILO. 2019. “<a href="https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@dgreports/@dcomm/@publ/documents/publication/wcms_711919.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Working on a Warmer Planet: The Impact of Heat Stress on Labour Productivity and Decent Work</a>.” Geneva: International Labour Organization.</div></div>`},{_key:"c17",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller",title:"40 percent of the global population is exposed to heatwaves",subtitle:`Areas and share of population exposed to heatwaves<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1968324149_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1968324149_1'/><div class="details">A heatwave occurs when the 5-day average maximum Wet-Bulb Globe Temperature (WBGT) exceeds 33 degrees.</div></div>`,source:'<a href="https://climateknowledgeportal.worldbank.org/" target="_blank" rel="external">World Bank Climate Change Portal</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Share of population exposed to heatwaves and location of areas exposed to heatwaves",data:"1b0tjeAlBqbSgpOKmVE4xMafDRNvsUWG7_C_jDMsorFI",anchorKey:"c17",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,weatherEventToggleText:"Exposed areas",legend_title:"Population exposed",legend_units:"%",gallery_thumbnail:"climate_sdg13_effects_of_climate_change_scroller_heatwaves",scenes:[{_key:"k8189",type:"scene",id:"heatwave__sat__wld",text:`The impact of <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>heatwaves</strong></span> on the human body depends on other factors, such as humidity and wind. So, instead of using a simple temperature measure, we use the Wet-Bulb Globe Temperature (WBGT), which better captures the effect of temperature on the human body. If the WBGT exceeds 33°C, it becomes difficult for healthy humans to maintain a normal body temperature, leading to increased mortality and hospitalizations. The capacity to undertake physical work is also halved.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 10"><label for='reference_10_2_1728418124' aria-hidden="true">10</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_10_2_1728418124'/><div class="details">Hill, R, Gorgulu, N, Brunckhorst, B, Nguyen, M, Hallegatte, S and Naikal, E. 2025. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">One-fifth of the World’s Population at High Risk from C</a>l<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">imate-related Hazards.” </a>(Preprint). Research Square.</div></div> `},{_key:"k8200",type:"scene",id:"heatwave__sat__bbox",text:'<span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>Heatwaves</strong></span> are mostly prevalent in the tropics. More than three-quarters of the global population exposed to heatwaves live in four populous countries in Asia: India, China, Pakistan, and Bangladesh. '},{_key:"k8207",type:"scene",id:"heatwave__vec__wld",text:'Globally, 40 percent of the population is exposed to <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>heatwaves</strong></span>. The share of people exposed to <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>heatwaves</strong></span> is especially high in countries around the Equator in Africa, South-East Asia and the Arabian peninsula.'}]},{_key:"k8260",type:"text",text:'If we overlay exposures to <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)"><strong>floods</strong></span>,  <span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)"><strong>agricultural droughts</strong></span>, <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)"><strong>cyclones </strong></span> or <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(170,0,0)"><strong>heatwaves</strong></span> with population density, 4.5 billion people—or around 57 percent of the world’s population—is exposed to at least one climate hazard. No country has no people exposed to one of the four hazards, but the share of people exposed varies from  <1–100 percent of a country’s population.'},{_key:"c19",type:"vis",graphic:"sdg13_effects_of_climate_change",title:"57 percent of the world’s population is exposed to climate-related hazards",subtitle:"Share of population exposed to at least one climate-related hazard",source:'<a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Share of population exposed to at least one climate hazard",data:"1b0tjeAlBqbSgpOKmVE4xMafDRNvsUWG7_C_jDMsorFI",anchorKey:"c19",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,overlay:"total_population",layer_toggle_label_on:"Total",layer_toggle_label_off:"Share",scale_title:"Percentage of population exposed to climate-related hazards",scale_title_pop:"Total number of people exposed to climate-related hazards",button_total:"Total population",button_share:"Share of population",scale_title_sha:"Percentage of population exposed to climate effects",legend_title:"Percentage of population exposed to climate effects",legend_units:"%",textParagraph:'Exposure to <span data-color-id="cat1" class="cat1"><strong>floods</strong></span>, <span data-color-id="cat2" class="cat2"><strong>agricultural droughts</strong></span>, <span data-color-id="cat3" class="cat3"><strong>cyclones </strong></span>and <span data-color-id="cat8" class="cat8"><strong>heatwaves </strong></span>depends on a country’s geographical location. In 44 countries, the whole population is exposed to at least one climate hazard.',keyFact:"4.5 billion",keyFactLabel:"people are exposed to climate-related hazards"},{_key:"k8299",type:"subhed",text:"2.8 billion people are highly vulnerable "},{_key:"k8322",type:"text",text:'Mapping climate-related hazards is only half the task; the other half is understanding people’s vulnerability to such climate hazards. The loss of income or damage to property caused by a <span data-color-id="cat3" class="cat3"><strong>cyclone</strong></span>, <span data-color-id="cat8" class="cat8"><strong>heatwave</strong></span>, <span data-color-id="cat2" class="cat2"><strong>drought</strong></span>, or <span data-color-id="cat1" class="cat1"><strong>flood </strong></span>may be a small inconvenience for some, but a long‑term livelihood shock for others. The impact depends on people’s ability and capacity to cope, adapt, and recover. In other words, climate risk is shaped not just by exposure, but also by vulnerability.'},{_key:"k8370",type:"text",text:`The World Bank has identified seven dimensions to assess a person’s general level of vulnerability, grouped into physical and economic vulnerability: physical vulnerability is measured by people’s access to <strong>clean water</strong>, <strong>electricity</strong>, <strong>services and markets</strong>;  economic vulnerability is measured by <strong>income</strong>,<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1976367852_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1976367852_1'/><div class="details">See also the <a href="https://data360.worldbank.org/en/atlas/extreme-poverty" target="_blank" rel="external">chapter on extreme poverty</a>.</div></div> <strong>education level</strong>, and access to <strong>social protection</strong> and financial services (<strong>financial inclusion</strong>).<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 10"><label for='reference_10_3_1728418124' aria-hidden="true">10</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_10_3_1728418124'/><div class="details">Hill, R, Gorgulu, N, Brunckhorst, B, Nguyen, M, Hallegatte, S and Naikal, E. 2025. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">One-fifth of the World’s Population at High Risk from C</a>l<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">imate-related Hazards.” </a>(Preprint). Research Square.</div></div> Individuals are considered to live in highly vulnerable conditions if they score below specific thresholds on at least one of these seven dimensions—for example, if their income falls below the extreme poverty line of $3 a day.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 10"><label for='reference_10_4_1728418124' aria-hidden="true">10</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_10_4_1728418124'/><div class="details">Hill, R, Gorgulu, N, Brunckhorst, B, Nguyen, M, Hallegatte, S and Naikal, E. 2025. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">One-fifth of the World’s Population at High Risk from C</a>l<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">imate-related Hazards.” </a>(Preprint). Research Square.</div></div> `},{_key:"k8371",type:"factoid",title:"People identified as living in highly vulnerable conditions",number:"2.8",units:"billion ",label:"globally in 2024"},{_key:"k8383",type:"text",text:`More than 1-in-3 people globally are considered highly vulnerable in at least one dimension, and many  experience vulnerability across several dimensions. Let’s look at how many people are in vulnerable conditions in each country and which dimensions of vulnerability they are experiencing.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_289290028_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_289290028_1'/><div class="details">In this graphic, we restrict ourselves to the six dimensions of vulnerability that come from household surveys. The seventh dimension is <strong>access to services and markets</strong>, which is measured using spatial data. As it is not possible to combine the spatial data with the survey data, we could not assess the overlap of this dimension with the six others. But spatial vulnerability affects only 44 million of the world’s 2.8 billion vulnerable people.</div></div>`},{_key:"c25",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_icon_matrix",title:"People can be highly vulnerable for several different reasons",subtitle:"Number and share of vulnerable people, by dimension, 2024",source:'<a href="https://scorecard.worldbank.org/en/data/indicator-detail/EN_CLM_VULN?orgCode=ALL&refareatype=REGION&refareacode=ACW&age=_T&disability=_T&sex=_T" target="_blank" rel="external">World Bank Scorecard Indicator</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"Figure showing the number of vulnerable people globally as well as by region and country. The figure also breaks down the vulnerability by six of its dimensions, showing the share of the population classified as vulnerable due to lack of access to clean water, electricity or financial services or lack of income, education, or social protection.",data:"1jnqFJhuylAsTw4s3XqgNSyLhIP4xF_nono5w93QKnSQ",anchorKey:"c25",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,label__x_axis:"Each group is 1000 squares",label__legend:"1 square =$x people",label__vul_atleastone:"Vulnerable population (in at least one dimension)",label__vul_inc:"Income vulnerability",label__vul_educ:"Education vulnerability",label__vul_fin:"Financial inclusion vulnerability",label__vul_sp:"Social protection vulnerability",label__vul_elec:"Electricity vulnerability",label__vul_water:"Water vulnerability",keyFact:"2.8 billion",keyFactLabel:"people live in highly vulnerable conditions globally",scenes:[{_key:"k8385",type:"scene",id:"multi__grid__vuln__wld",text:'Each row has 1,000 squares, and each individual square represents 8 million people. More than one-third of the  <span data-color-id="WLD" class="WLD"><strong>world’s population</strong></span>— 2.8 billion people—are vulnerable in at least one dimension, and almost half of these are vulnerable in two or more dimensions. <strong>Lack of social protection</strong> is the most common source of vulnerability (20 percent of the world’s population). <strong>Lack of financial inclusion</strong>, <strong>lack of education</strong>, and living in <strong>extreme poverty due to low income</strong> are the next leading causes of vulnerability. '},{_key:"k8410",type:"scene",id:"multi__grid__vuln__nac",text:'Levels of vulnerability range from 1.7–94 percent across regions. In <span data-color-id="NAC" class="NAC"><strong>North America</strong></span>, only 1.7 percent of the population is vulnerable. These are people living in extreme poverty (<strong>lack of income</strong>) or those who <strong>lack basic education</strong>.'},{_key:"k8426",type:"scene",id:"multi__grid__vuln__ssf",text:'In <span data-color-id="SSF" class="SSF"><strong>Sub-Saharan Africa,</strong></span> a staggering 94 percent of the population is vulnerable. <strong>Lack of social protection</strong> impacts almost 3 in 4 people and <strong>lack of access to electricity</strong> or <strong>extreme poverty</strong> affects almost half the population. '},{_key:"k8447",type:"scene",id:"multi__explore",text:"Select an economy or region to see the share of population and number of people that are vulnerable and the dimensions of vulnerability that are driving it."}]},{_key:"k8467",type:"subhed",text:"1.5 billion people are at high risk from climate-related hazards"},{_key:"k8474",type:"text",text:"When we combine exposure to climate-related hazards with vulnerabilities, we can identify people who are <strong>at high risk from climate-related hazards</strong>. People are considered at high risk when they are exposed to any of the four climate-related hazards and are considered highly vulnerable. "},{_key:"k8475",type:"factoid",title:"At high risk from climate-related hazards",number:"1.5",units:"billion",label:"People globally are both exposed to climate-related hazards and living in highly vulnerable conditions"},{_key:"c29",type:"vis",graphic:"sdg13_regional_exposure_vuln",title:"The number of people at high risk from climate-related hazards varies by region",subtitle:"Share of population at high risk from climate-related hazards in 2024",source:'<a href="https://scorecard.worldbank.org/en/data/indicator-detail/EN_CLM_VULN?orgCode=ALL&refareatype=REGION&refareacode=ACW&age=_T&disability=_T&sex=_T" target="_blank" rel="external">World Bank Scorecard Indicator</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"Map showing the share of regional populations defined as being at high risk from climate-related hazards in 2024.",data:"1jnqFJhuylAsTw4s3XqgNSyLhIP4xF_nono5w93QKnSQ",anchorKey:"c29",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,label__total_population:"Total population",label__population_exposed:"Population exposed",label__population_exposed_at_risk:"Population exposed and highly vulnerable",label__legend_units:"Millions","activeScene.id":"map_2024",textParagraph:'On this map, the size of the grey circles is proportional to the size of the population of the seven World Bank regions. The blue circles show the size of the population exposed to climate-related hazards. The red circle shows the size of the population at high risk (both exposed to climate-related hazards and in vulnerable conditions). In <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(52,167,242)">North America</span> less than 1 percent of the population (approximately 0.6 million people) are at high risk. In contrast, more than a third of the people living in the <span style="color: rgb(255,255,255); background-color: rgb(102,74,182)">Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan and Pakistan</span> and <span style="color: rgb(4,1,16); background-color: rgb(255,152,0)">Sub-Saharan Africa</span> regions are at high risk.'},{_key:"k8502",type:"text",text:"Almost 20 percent of the global population is at high risk from climate-related hazards. Across countries, the population at high risk can differ widely due to differences in either exposure or vulnerability."},{_key:"c31",type:"scroller",graphic:"sdg13_icon_matrix",title:"Population at high risk depends on both exposure and vulnerability",subtitle:"Comparing countries with similar levels of exposure or vulnerability",source:'<a href="https://scorecard.worldbank.org/en/data/indicator-detail/EN_CLM_VULN?orgCode=ALL&refareatype=REGION&refareacode=ACW&age=_T&disability=_T&sex=_T" target="_blank" rel="external">World Bank Scorecard Indicator</a>, <a href="https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">Hill et al.</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"The figure shows comparisons between countries, where the share of people at high risk differs greatly, despite having similar levels of exposure or vulnerability. ",data:"1jnqFJhuylAsTw4s3XqgNSyLhIP4xF_nono5w93QKnSQ",anchorKey:"c31",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!0,label__x_axis:"Each group is 1,000 squares",label__legend:"1 square =$x people",label__highly_vulnerable:"Vulnerable in at least 1 dimension",label__at_risk:"At high risk",label__not_vulnerable:"Not vulnerable",label__exposed:"Exposed to climate-related hazards",label__not_exposed:"Not exposed",gallery_thumbnail:"climate_sdg13_icon_matrix",keyFact:"1.5 billion",keyFactLabel:"people are exposed to climate-related hazards and living in highly vulnerable conditions",scenes:[{_key:"k8504",type:"scene",id:"mono__grid__vul__bfa_ken",text:"<strong>Burkina Faso</strong> and <strong>Kenya</strong> have similar levels of <strong>vulnerability</strong>. In both countries, around 9 in 10 people  are identified as vulnerable in at least one dimension."},{_key:"k8520",type:"scene",id:"mono__diff__vul_exp__bfa_ken",text:"But <strong>exposure</strong> to climate hazards is three times higher in <strong>Burkina Faso</strong>. Combining the number of vulnerable and exposed people, only 1 in 4 people in <strong>Kenya</strong> are at high risk from climate hazards, compared with 3 in 4 people in <strong>Burkina Faso</strong>. "},{_key:"k8541",type:"scene",id:"mono__grid__exp__phl_vnm",text:'Similarly, in <span data-color-id="EAS" class="EAS">East Asia and Pacific</span>, almost 9 in 10 people in the <strong>Philippines</strong> and <strong>Vietnam</strong> are <strong>exposed</strong> to climate hazards. '},{_key:"k8561",type:"scene",id:"mono__diff__exp_vul__phl_vnm",text:"But despite their similar <strong>exposure</strong> profiles, slightly more than half the population of the <strong>Philippines</strong> is at risk, compared to around 20 percent in <strong>Vietnam</strong>. This is due to the differing levels of vulnerability. In the <strong>Philippines</strong>, 3 in 5 people are vulnerable, while  in <strong>Vietnam</strong>,  it is 1 in 5 people."}]},{_key:"k8603",type:"subhed",text:"Mitigation, reducing vulnerabilities, and building resilience"},{_key:"k8606",type:"text",text:"Climate-related hazards can threaten people’s health, livelihoods, food security, and safety, deepening existing inequalities. People at high risk are those who are exposed to climate-related hazards—such as floods, agricultural droughts, cyclones and heatwaves—and also live in vulnerable conditions. In 2024, 4.5 billion people were exposed to climate-related hazards and 2.8 billion lived in highly vulnerable conditions. Looking at the overlap between these two groups, 1.5 billion people were at high risk from climate-related hazards."},{_key:"k8616",type:"text",text:`To reduce the number of people at high risk, governments need to pursue both climate mitigation and adaptation. Taking measures to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and limit future warming can help slow the growth of climate-related hazards and exposure to them. Governments are also working to reduce people’s vulnerabilities and increase their resilience towards climate-related hazards. The World Bank’s <a href="https://datacatalog.worldbank.org/int/search/dataset/0066854/adaptation_and_resilience_readiness_data" target="_blank" rel="external"><strong>Adaptation and Resilience Readiness Assessment Tool</strong></a> assesses how well countries are positioned to design and implement effective climate change adaptation and resilience strategies by evaluating key policies, institutions, and actions across multiple pillars and indicators.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 5"><label for='reference_5_2_1449623743' aria-hidden="true">5</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_5_2_1449623743'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2024. “<a href="https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/rising-to-the-challenge-climate-adaptation-resilience" target="_blank" rel="external">Rising to the Challenge: Success Stories and Strategies for Achieving Climate Adaptation and Resilience</a>.” Washington DC: World Bank. </div></div>`},{_key:"k8629",type:"text",text:"This tool, first applied in 17 Caribbean economies, has guided adaptation efforts elsewhere, too. For example, World Bank–supported programs have helped <strong>Zambia</strong> and <strong>Brazil</strong> shift from reactive to proactive resilience building, contributing to substantial declines in vulnerability and the number of people at high risk from climate-related hazards. Experiences like these show that reducing climate risk is possible, even as exposure increases. Continued investments in adaptation, resilience, and inclusive development can further reduce vulnerabilities and protect lives and livelihoods from shocks, including climate‑related shocks."}],t=[{text:'IPCC. 2022. “<a href="https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg2/" target="_blank" rel="external">Climate Change 2022: Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability. Contribution of Working Group II to the Sixth Assessment Report</a>.” Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.',index:0},{text:'FAO. “<a href="https://www.fao.org/land-water/water/drought/droughtandag/en/" target="_blank" rel="external">Drought and agriculture.</a>” Land & Water.  Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations. ',index:1},{text:'ILO. 2019. “<a href="https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@dgreports/@dcomm/@publ/documents/publication/wcms_711919.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Working on a Warmer Planet: The Impact of Heat Stress on Labour Productivity and Decent Work</a>.” Geneva: International Labour Organization.',index:2},{text:'Abeygunawardena, P, Vyas, Y, Knill, P, Foy, T, Harrold, M, Steele, P, Tanner, T, Hirsch, D, Oosterman, M, Rooimans, J, Debois, M, Lamin, M, Liptow, H, Mausolf, E, Verheyen, R, Agrawala, S, Caspary, G, Paris, R, Kashyap, A, Sharma, A, Mathur, A, Sharma, M and Sperling, F. 2010. “<a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/534871468155709473" target="_blank" rel="external">Poverty and Climate Change: Reducing the Vulnerability of the Poor through Adaptation.</a>“ Washington DC: World Bank.',index:3},{text:'World Bank. 2024. “<a href="https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/rising-to-the-challenge-climate-adaptation-resilience" target="_blank" rel="external">Rising to the Challenge: Success Stories and Strategies for Achieving Climate Adaptation and Resilience</a>.” Washington DC: World Bank. ',index:4},{text:'UNEP. 2020. “<a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/story/how-climate-change-making-record-breaking-floods-new-normal" target="_blank" rel="external">How Climate Change is Making Record-breaking Floods the New Normal</a><a href="https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/story/how-climate-change-making-record-breaking-floods-new-normal" target="_blank" rel="external">.</a>” United Nations Environment Program climate action story, March 3.',index:5},{text:'Rentschler, J, Salhab, M and Jafino, B A. 2022. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-30727-4" target="_blank" rel="external">Flood Exposure and Poverty in 188 Countries.</a>” Nature Communications 13: 3527.',index:6},{text:'FAO. <a href="https://www.fao.org/giews/earthobservation/asis/index_1.jsp?lang=en" target="_blank" rel="external">“Agricultural Stress Index System (ASIS).</a>” Earth Observation. Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations.',index:7},{text:'Van Hoolst, R, Eerens, H, Haesen, D, Royer, A, Bydekerke, L, Rojas, O, Li, Y and Racionzer, P. 2016. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/01431161.2015.1126378" target="_blank" rel="external">FAO’s AVHRR-based Agricultural Stress Index System (ASIS) for Global Drought Monitoring</a>.” International Journal of Remote Sensing 37(2): 418–439.',index:8},{text:'Hill, R, Gorgulu, N, Brunckhorst, B, Nguyen, M, Hallegatte, S and Naikal, E. 2025. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">One-fifth of the World’s Population at High Risk from C</a>l<a href="https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5302969/v2" target="_blank" rel="external">imate-related Hazards.” </a>(Preprint). Research Square.',index:9},{text:'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mfl/saffirsimpson" target="_blank" rel="external">“Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Scale</a><a href="https://www.weather.gov/mfl/saffirsimpson" target="_blank" rel="external">”</a>.  ',index:10},{text:'WMO. 2025. “<a href="https://wmo.int/media/news/category-5-hurricane-melissa-hits-jamaica-and-caribbean-islands" target="_blank" rel="external">Category 5 Hurricane Melissa hits Jamaica and Caribbean islands.</a>” World Meteorological Organization News, October 28.',index:11},{text:'WHO. “<a href="https://www.who.int/health-topics/heatwaves" target="_blank" rel="external">Heatwaves.</a>” World Health Organization.  ',index:12}],r={config:e,content:a,references:t};export{e as config,a as content,r as default,t as references};
